 in New York, Feb. 26, 2026. (AP Photo/Patrick Sison, File)†dims.apnews.com L1785: \nL1786: Pages from the Anthropic website and the company’s logos are displayed on a computer screen in New York, Feb. 26, 2026. (AP Photo/Patrick Sison, File)\nL1787: \nL1788: cite180†License this photo†newsroom.ap.org L1789: \nL1790: By  cite181†KAITLYN HUAMANI L1791: \nL1792: \nL1793: \nL1794: cite182†Leer en español L1795: Add AP News on Google Add AP News as your preferred source to see more of our stories on Google. Share\nL1796: \nL1797: cite183†Image: Comments†dims.apnews.com L1798: \nL1799: Share\nL1800: \nL1801:   * cite184†Facebook†www.facebook.com L1802:   * Copy\nL1803: \nL1804: Link copied\nL1805: \nL1806:   * Print\nL1807:   * cite185†Email L1808:   * cite186†X†twitter.com L1809:   * cite187†LinkedIn L1810:   * cite188†Bluesky†bsky.app L1811:   * cite189†Flipboard†share.flipboard.com L1812:   * cite190†Pinterest†pinterest.com L1813:   * cite191†Reddit†www.reddit.com L1814: cite192†Anthropic’s Claude is helping the company develop the next, more intelligent version of the model, the cite128†artificial intelligence lab said in an announcement Thursday.\nL1815: \nL1816: Claude is leading 26% of Anthropic’s model research and development, which the company said means it can complete most of a given task “end-to-end from a high-level prompt” while still being under human supervision. The model is not yet working completely autonomously.\nL1817: Still, about 90% of the company’s research and development is done in “collaboration” with Claude, which Anthropic said means the model can do “large chunks of work under close human direction.”\nL1818: \nL1819: The announcement came as some leading figures in AI, led in large part by cite193†Anthropic CEO Dario Amodei , are calling for a cite193†slowdown in the technology’s development over safety concerns.\nL1820: As leaders consider pacing AI’s development, “we should do everything possible to minimize the gap between what frontier labs know and what the public knows,” the company said in a blog post. “This means better measuring the development of AI, reporting on it publicly, and giving society an opportunity to decide how to use this information.”\nL1821: The company said that models accelerating their own development could make it “more challenging for humans to understand or control these systems.” It argued that sharing these metrics could lead to better understanding of how close leading AI labs are to reaching recursive self-improvement, or a model’s ability to autonomously build its successor.\nL1822: Anthropic also urged other AI developers to share similar metrics on a regular basis, encouraging the use of a public methodology so the numbers could be compared over time, and potentially across labs.\nL1823: \nL1824: Related Stories\nL1825: \nL1826: cite72†Worries about an AI internet takeover gain new urgency among doomsday scenarios L1827: \nL1828: 4 MIN READ\nL1829: \nL1830: cite72†63 L1831: \nL1832: cite194†China and the US are competing for AI dominance but have shared concerns over safety L1833: \nL1834: 5 MIN READ\nL1835: cite131†Lawsuit says Anthropic, OpenAI, SpaceXAI and Google made illegal agreement on AI slowdown L1836: \nL1837: 3 MIN READ\nL1838: \nL1839: cite131†395 L1840: It was unclear from Anthropic’s disclosure how close the company believes it is to achieving recursive self-improvement, but the pace at which Claude has increasingly contributed to research and development is notable. The portion of work Claude “leads,” or does in large part while remaining under human supervision, was none in February. Six months later, it was leading a quarter of research and development work, reaching that benchmark in August.\nL1841: \nL1842: Read More\nL1843: The company also shared details of agent oversight measures it has in place, noting that there were approximately 30,000 agents doing research and engineering work as of August. Oversight measures are crucial for seeing how often agent misbehavior is detected by monitoring systems, the company said. Anthropic recently committed to setting up external third-party evaluators who will be embedded within the company to monitor safety efforts.\nL1844: An Anthropic researcher kicked off much of the recent dialogue around AI safety when he resigned last week with a cite195†dire warning about the threats the technology poses to humanity. Amodei, OpenAI CEO Sam Altman, Elon Musk and other tech leaders have since cite196†supported the idea of slowing down development , but other tech leaders and cite197†President Donald Trump have pushed back.\nL1845: \nL1846: cite181†KAITLYN HUAMANI L1847: \nL1848: Huamani covers social media and internet culture for The Associated Press.\nL1849: \nL1850: cite198†mailto L1851: Most read\nL1852: \nL1853:   1. cite10†A nation renamed stands up at the UN: ‘Say our name. Naoero.’ L1854: \nL1855: 2 MIN READ\nL1856: \nL1857: cite10†121 L1858: \nL1859:   2. cite25†Trump signs US-Denmark-Greenland agreement to bolster Arctic security after prolonged tensions L1860: \nL1861: 5 MIN READ\nL1862: \nL1863: cite25†283 L1864: \nL1865:   3. cite87†FACT FOCUS: Trump made multiple false and misleading claims in front of the UN General Assembly L1866: \nL1867: 4 MIN READ\nL1868: \nL1869: cite87†744 L1870: \nL1871:   4. cite26†‘Looksmaxxing’ influencer known as ‘Clavicular’ is charged with rape in Massachusetts L1872: \nL1873: 2 MIN READ\nL1874: \nL1875: cite26†133 L1876:   5. cite199†Hurricane Polo churns off Mexico’s Pacific coast as ‘one of the strongest storms ever’ L1877: \nL1878: 4 MIN READ\nL1879: \nL1880: cite199†37 L1881: \nL1882: The Associated Press is an independent global news organization dedicated to factual reporting. Founded in 1846, AP today remains the most trusted source of fast, accurate, unbiased news in all formats and the essential provider of the t